Actually, that's correct! `x == 0` as a boolean is true (1) when x == 0, false (0) otherwise. So `zext (x == 0)` is correct.

But wait, the original was `select (x == 0), (x + 1), 0`. When `x == 0`, it returns `x + 1 = 1`. When `x != 0`, it returns 0. So it returns 1 when x == 0, 0 otherwise. That's exactly `x == 0` as a 0/1 integer. So the optimization is correct.

Hmm, let me think of a case where the optimization might be wrong. What about `select (x == 0), (x | 1), 0`? When `x == 0`, `0 | 1 = 1`. When `x != 0`, returns 0. So it's `select (x == 0), 1, 0` which is `x == 0`. But `x | 1` is not constant when `x == 0`? Actually, when `x == 0`, `0 | 1 = 1`, which is constant.

What about `select (x == 0), (x & ~1), 0`? When `x == 0`, `0 & ~1 = 0`. When `x != 0`, returns 0. So it's always 0. But `x & ~1` is not constant when `x == 0`? Actually, when `x == 0`, `0 & ~1 = 0`, which is constant.

